//Shallow Copy

Shallow copy means copying an object's data as it is, including copying the address stored in a pointer.

Example:

class Student {
public:
    int* age;

    Student(int a) {
        age = new int(a);
    }
};

Suppose:

Student s1(20);
Student s2 = s1;

With a shallow copy:

s1.age ──→ [20]
             ↑
s2.age ──────┘

Both s1.age and s2.age point to the same memory location.

So if:

*s2.age = 25;

then:

*s1.age

also becomes 25.

The problem

Both objects are pointing to the same dynamically allocated memory.

This can cause problems such as double deletion when both destructors try to free the same memory.

Simple definition

Shallow copy = copying the pointer/address itself, so multiple objects can point to the same underlying data.

This becomes especially important when a class manages dynamic memory/resources.